Velocity, pressure and probe interference (@fd-velocity-probes: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @fluid-dynamics, #source "../sources/Experimental-Methods-in-Fluids-Mondal.lingua#r_4XFYEGQVB1EV4QF9BP17ME04Y4", #source "../sources/Gas-Dynamics-Kulkarni.lingua#r_34YGDV61X77JFYZH8X8NGDBS9W", #source "../sources/PIV-Uncertainty-Sciacchitano.lingua#r_4PHTCFRTAGSCNV1TK192VZHGQ6", #fluid-dynamics "../../../inquiry/metrology/applications/Dynamic-measurements-and-time-series.lingua#r_7VY283E2ZMNS2JTQ9Y7DNBW6KX") { r_350KWVSRBR55T06BP45HY4B22C
**Read:** [Experimental Methods in Fluid Mechanics — Pranab K. Mondal](../sources/Experimental-Methods-in-Fluids-Mondal.lingua#r_4XFYEGQVB1EV4QF9BP17ME04Y4), pressure, flow-angle, velocity and response lessons; [Gas Dynamics — Vinayak Kulkarni](../sources/Gas-Dynamics-Kulkarni.lingua#r_34YGDV61X77JFYZH8X8NGDBS9W), diagnostics and compressible-flow measurement; [Uncertainty quantification in particle image velocimetry — Sciacchitano](../sources/PIV-Uncertainty-Sciacchitano.lingua#r_4PHTCFRTAGSCNV1TK192VZHGQ6), comparison of measurement and derived-field uncertainty.

A Pitot-type measurement infers velocity from a pressure relation under stated flow and probe conditions. For low-speed incompressible motion, a suitable total-minus-static pressure estimate gives U≈√(2Δp/ρ), but yaw, shear, turbulence, blockage and calibration modify the inference. At compressible speed, use the appropriate stagnation and shock relations.

Hot-wire and hot-film methods infer motion through convective heat transfer and an electrical response. Their calibration can depend on temperature, direction, fluid properties, contamination and frequency. Optical methods have another chain of assumptions. Choosing an instrument therefore starts from the required component, scale, bandwidth and environment rather than from a preferred output format.

Quantify probe size relative to velocity gradients and compare a traverse with an independent bulk-flow constraint. Explain what a one-component measurement cannot identify. Preserve probe position and orientation, calibration before/after a run, uncertainty in density and the response model used to turn signals into velocity.

**Continue:** [Changing signals — response correction, sampling, and dependent noise](../../../inquiry/metrology/applications/Dynamic-measurements-and-time-series.lingua#r_7VY283E2ZMNS2JTQ9Y7DNBW6KX).

